\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The Hermitian convention can retain extra Wilson zeros}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Set #{d=1}, #{N=4}, #{a=r=1}, trivial links and
#{F=\mathbb C}. The chosen spin space is #{S=\mathbb C^2},
with gamma matrix
##{X=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}.}
The free skew Dirac block is #{D_1(p)=iX\sin p}.
Replacing it by the Hermitian operator #{iD_1} and then adding
the same Wilson term gives the block
##{H_W(p)=-X\sin p+(1-\cos p)I.}
This is a different operator from \ref{fdip-009V}.}

\p{The normalized vectors #{(1,1)/\sqrt2} and
#{(1,-1)/\sqrt2} are #{X}-eigenvectors of eigenvalues
#{+1} and #{-1}. Thus the two eigenvalues of #{H_W(p)} are
#{1-\cos p\mp\sin p}. On the four allowed momenta:
##{\begin{array}{c|cc|c}
p&X=+1&X=-1&\dim\ker H_W(p)\\\hline
0&0&0&2\\
\pi /2&0&2&1\\
\pi&2&2&0\\
3\pi /2&2&0&1
\end{array}}
The total kernel dimension is four. The skew Wilson operator
#{D_1+L_1/2} instead has kernel dimension two, consisting of
constant fields. Tensoring this example with an internal space
of dimension #{q} multiplies both counts by #{q}.}

\p{The difference is elementary: a real scalar can cancel a
real eigenvalue of the Hermitian first difference. For the skew
first difference the squared norm is a sum of nonnegative
squares, as in the preceding proof. Adjoint conventions therefore
cannot be changed silently in a claim that the Wilson term
removes all nonzero-momentum zeros.}
